# TBIT-style probe: ECN CE-marked data triggers ECE on the response
# ACK.
#
# RFC 3168 §6.1.2: when a receiver gets an IP packet with the CE
# (Congestion Experienced, 0b11) codepoint, it MUST echo ECE on
# subsequent ACKs until it observes a CWR from the sender. This is
# the receiver-side half of ECN feedback that lets the SENDER react
# to congestion without dropping any packet.
#
# Setup: full ECN-negotiated handshake (ECT(0) flowing), then a
# CE-marked data segment arrives. We should respond with an ACK
# carrying ECE.

--connect 10.0.0.1:49152 10.0.0.2:80
+0 > SEW 0:0(0) win 65535 <mss 1460, wscale 5, sackOK, TS val * ecr 0>
+.005 < SE. 0:0(0) ack 1 win 32768 <mss 1460, wscale 0, sackOK, TS val 1 ecr *>
# Pure ACKs MUST be Not-ECT (RFC 3168 §6.1.4) even on an
# ECN-negotiated connection — only data-bearing first transmissions
# carry ECT(0).
+0 > . 1:1(0) ack 1 ecn NotECT <TS val * ecr 1>

# Peer sends a 100-byte data segment with the CE codepoint set —
# simulating an ECN-aware router in the path marking the packet as
# experiencing congestion.
+.005 < P. 1:101(100) ack 1 win 32768 ecn CE <TS val 2 ecr *>

# Our response ACK MUST carry ECE (in TCP flags). At the IP layer a
# pure ACK is Not-ECT per RFC 3168 §6.1.4 (a CE mark on an ACK-only
# packet is congestion feedback the ACK flow cannot react to). The
# ACK should be at rcv_nxt=101.
#
# Delayed-ACK fires at ~200ms by default, so we wait past that to
# guarantee the ACK is emitted (one data segment alone wouldn't
# trigger an immediate ACK — that requires either two segments
# back-to-back or the delayed-ACK timer expiry).
+.250 > E. 1:1(0) ack 101 ecn NotECT <TS val * ecr 2>
